#SYSTEMDYNAMICS

BLURB
Use the tools of phase spaces--including attractors, critical points, and basins--to describe and predict the ways a complex system changes over time.

PITCH
All roads lead to Rome because Rome is an attractor in the classical-era transportation phase space.

PARAGRAPH DESCRIPTION
Complex systems change over time. Phase spaces can be used to describe and predict these changes, providing the foundation for planning interventions. The dimensions of a phase space (similar to axes on a graph) describe measurable features of the system that can increase or decrease over time. Attractors are locations on the phase space that represent stable equilibria: a system at that combination of values on the dimensions is likely to stay at that combination. Attractors can be described as surrounded by basins of attraction, representing a range of values on the dimensions that are likely to move towards the attractor in the basin. This implies critical points at the edges of each basin: a system is unlikely to move to a specific attractor unless it reaches a critical point on the boundary of that attractor’s basin, but if it does pass that critical point then it is likely to move to the attractor.

